# Frontend Project Conventions

Decisions already made for this project. Follow them, and ask the user
before changing one.

## Stack

- React 19, Vite 8, TypeScript (strict).
- Styling: CSS Modules plus design tokens in `src/styles/tokens.css`.
- Runtime dependencies besides React: `react-markdown` and `remark-gfm`,
  used only to render agent answers.
- No UI kit, router, state manager, test runner, or linter is installed.

## Where Code Goes

- `src/features/<feature>/` — one feature: its components, hook, and
  pure logic. Existing: `selection`, `diagnosis`, `chat`, `tickets`.
- `src/components/` — shared UI only: `AppLayout`, `Button`, `Panel`,
  `Notice`, and the page-level notices (`MissingEnvNotice`,
  `InsecureUrlNotice`, `OfflineNotice`).
- `src/api/` — `http.ts` (`postJson`, `ApiError`) and one client per service.
- `src/config/env.ts` — the only place that reads `import.meta.env`.
- `src/config/heuristics.ts` — team thresholds: low confidence,
  deviation-strength bands, ticket priority inputs. Change them there
  and update `frontend/README.md`. The UI shows them to the mechanic.
- `src/config/manuals.ts` — repair manuals per engine model with page
  counts, taken from the TZ. The only source of manual names; `aliases`
  hold the PDF file names from requirements table 6.1 that the agent may
  use, while the ticket always gets the TZ designation.
- `src/types/` — API contracts and fleet data shapes.
- `src/pages/` — composition only: wires features together.

## Diagnosis Result

- The mechanic learns the result from the AI agent's conclusion. The
  agent's workflow fetches the ML result itself; the app sends it
  nothing from the ML response.
- The app still calls `POST /diagnose`, but only for the ticket: the
  response is never shown in the feed. It decides whether a ticket is
  offered and supplies the fault code and the inputs of the fallback
  priority calculation.
- The low-confidence threshold is 0.60 (requirements v2). The threshold
  and the reading rules are shown in the UI by `DiagnosisRules`, built
  from the same constants as the calculation.
- The app's own verdict card is switched off by the user's decision
  ("for now"). `DiagnosisVerdict.tsx` and `buildVerdict` are kept but
  not rendered. Do not bring the card back or delete it without asking.
- Reliability rules live in `features/diagnosis/assessment.ts` and
  depend on `status`, `signal_quality`, and `confidence` only.
  `signal_quality = bad` is a failed analysis, same as `check_failed`,
  whatever the `status`. Never let `description` change a decision.
- Do not show F-codes (`F01`…`F14`) to the mechanic.

## Chat

- The conversation belongs to one vehicle/session pair. `Conversation`
  remounts its inner component by `key` when the selection changes, so
  hooks inside never track selection changes themselves.
- The agent's conclusion, answers, tickets, and request errors are feed
  messages. State transitions live in the pure reducer
  `features/chat/conversationState.ts`.
- The feed is `role="log"`: do not move focus to new messages, and pass
  `quiet` to any `Notice` rendered inside it.
- Everything for the AI agent goes through `api/agentClient.ts`
  (`sendAgentMessage`): one POST to the webhook in `VITE_AGENT_URL` with
  `message`, `vehicle_id`, `engine_model`, `session_id`, `client_id`.
  The diagnosis result is not sent, neither as a field nor in the text.
  The vehicle's history is not sent either: the agent owns it.
- Images: with attachments the same fields go as `multipart/form-data`
  strings plus one `files` part per image; without them the body stays
  JSON. Never set `Content-Type` by hand for `FormData` (the browser
  adds the boundary). `features/chat/imageAttachments.ts` validates,
  downsizes (1600 px, JPEG) and thus strips EXIF; images live in memory
  only (never `localStorage`), object URLs are revoked, and neither file
  data nor base64 goes to the console. Do not copy auth fields from
  other agents' pipes into this public repo.
- The diagnosis button calls ML, then sends the agent a request for a
  conclusion: the constant `ANALYSIS_REQUEST` in
  `features/chat/analysisRequest.ts`. Keep it short, by the user's
  decision: the agent has its own prompt with the answer rules, so do
  not add rules, thresholds, manual names, or an output format to the
  message. When the text changes, update `frontend/README.md` too.
- The ticket draft format (a fenced JSON block) is not requested by the
  app; it works only if the agent's own prompt asks for it.
- Only one agent request runs at a time: a conclusion and a question
  never overlap.
- Agent requests have no client-side timeout (`timeoutMs: null`), by the
  user's decision: a conclusion takes several minutes. ML and TOIR keep
  the default 15 s.
- `features/chat/agentAnswer.ts` splits an answer into text and an
  optional ticket draft (a fenced JSON block). Raw JSON never reaches
  the feed, even when the block cannot be parsed.
- A failed conclusion request offers a retry and no ticket. The ticket
  button is shown once, under the message returned by
  `findTicketAnchor` (the latest conclusion).
- `client_id` is the conversation id: created once per conversation in
  `useConversation`, so it changes whenever the conversation restarts.
- The agent's response format is not fixed. `agentClient.ts` accepts the
  variants listed in `frontend/README.md`; update both together.
- Keep the real webhook URL out of the repository: it lives only in the
  local `.env`; `.env.example` keeps `VITE_AGENT_URL` empty. The agent
  needs the company VPN, so verify agent code with faked responses.
- Voice input (`useSpeechInput`) puts recognized text into the field and
  never sends it. Hide the mic button when recognition is unavailable
  and say why.
- The mechanic's messages are rendered as plain text. Agent answers are
  rendered as Markdown by `features/chat/AgentMarkdown.tsx`
  (`react-markdown` + `remark-gfm`, approved by the user). Never add
  `rehype-raw` or `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`: HTML from an answer must
  stay text. Images are disallowed; links open in a new tab.
- `<think>` reasoning is split off in `agentAnswer.ts` and shown in a
  collapsed `<details>` above the answer, as plain text (the user's
  choice, as in Open WebUI). It never appears in the answer itself.

## Tickets

- A ticket is a feed message (`kind: 'ticket'`) rendered by
  `features/tickets/TicketCard`: form → review → created or cancelled.
  Only one ticket can be open at a time.
- Offer a ticket only when `canRequestTicket` is true for the ML
  result: a reliable anomaly with a known fault code. The agent's text
  or draft never enables a ticket on its own.
- The mechanic may start with a question instead of the diagnosis
  button. The first question silently calls `POST /diagnose` once per
  conversation (`loadDiagnosisInBackground` in `useConversation`; no
  pending flag, no error in the feed, retried by the next question) and
  stores the result in `ConversationState.diagnosis`. `findTicketAnchor`
  puts the button under the latest conclusion: a button-flow answer
  (`analysisOf`), else an answer with a draft, else the latest answer.
  Keep it a single button per conversation.
- `POST /tickets` is sent only from the review step, by the explicit
  confirm button, with exactly the request shown there. Editing or
  cancelling sends nothing.
- Never invent references. The manual comes from `config/manuals.ts`
  for the vehicle's engine; the page number comes from the agent's
  draft or is typed by the mechanic, and is checked against the
  manual's page count. No default page.
- The agent's draft is a suggestion. `applyAgentSuggestion` in
  `ticketDraft.ts` fills the form when the ticket is opened, drops
  references to other engines' manuals or to pages beyond the page
  count, and lists the dropped ones for the mechanic. The form says
  that the agent wrote the text.
- Priority and its justification come from the agent (`urgency` and
  `urgency_reason` in the draft, or a "Приоритет: …" line in the text);
  `resolvePriority` falls back to the app's own calculation and says so.
  The mechanic can change it, and the form says who chose what.
- A ticket without a manual reference is never sent (the TOIR schema
  needs one). When the agent gives no source, say so above the form: for
  "no procedure in the manual" the agent recommends manual troubleshooting
  and does not form a ticket (requirements v2, 8.2.2.4).
- Do not copy the ML `description` into the ticket: build the
  conclusion from structured fields only.
- The fallback priority comes from `features/tickets/priority.ts`
  together with its reasons; always show the reasons, including that
  node criticality and the vehicle's history are not part of it.
- A ticket number is shown only after a `201` with `ticket_id`. On
  timeout or an unreadable response say the outcome is unknown.
- Do not abort the ticket request on unmount: the number must reach
  the history even if the mechanic leaves the session.
- History lives in `ticketHistoryStore.ts` (`localStorage`, read with
  `useSyncExternalStore`). Do not name files so that they differ only
  by letter case: the build fails on Windows.

## Service Calls

- Service URLs come only from `.env` (`VITE_MOCK_ML_URL`,
  `VITE_MOCK_TOIR_URL`, `VITE_AGENT_URL`). No fallback URLs in code.
- API code throws `ApiError` with a `kind`; the feature maps `kind` to
  user-facing text. No UI strings in `src/api/`.
- `kind: 'offline'` means the device had no network when the request
  was sent, so the request did not leave. Never block a request because
  `navigator.onLine` is false: services on the same machine still answer.
- Stand builds read service URLs from `.env.production.local`. On an
  HTTPS page a non-loopback `http://` service URL is blocked by the
  browser; `findInsecureEnvVars` in `config/env.ts` reports it and
  `InsecureUrlNotice` shows it.
- Check every external response with a type guard before using it.
- A diagnosis result comes only from the ML service. `Session` holds just
  `session_id` and `checked_at` on purpose; do not read results from
  `vehicles.json`.

## PWA

- The app is installable and its shell opens offline. No PWA library:
  `public/manifest.webmanifest`, `public/icons/`, the service worker
  source `pwa/sw.js` and the build plugin `pwa/pwaPlugin.ts` (it
  prepends `BUILD_ID` and the file list and emits `dist/sw.js`).
  Registration is `src/pwa/registerServiceWorker.ts`, production only.
- The worker handles only same-origin GET requests and caches only the
  shell files. Never cache service responses (ML, TOIR, agent), never
  add them to the precache list, never intercept other origins or POST.
- A new file in `public/` that must work offline goes into
  `PUBLIC_SHELL_FILES` in `pwa/pwaPlugin.ts`; a wrong path breaks the
  worker install. Cache lookups use `ignoreVary` on purpose (the server
  sends `Vary: Origin`, module scripts carry `Origin`).
- The icons are drawn from `public/favicon.svg`; redraw them when the
  logo changes. Keep relative URLs in the manifest so `--base` works.
- Verify with `npm run build` and `npx vite preview`, then stop the
  server and reload: the shell must still open. Unregister the worker
  and clear caches afterwards.
- Do not add `vite-plugin-pwa`, Workbox, or other dependencies without
  the user's approval.

## Data Files

- `src/data/vehicles.json` and `src/data/fault_types.json` are byte-for-byte
  copies of `neurodvs_mock/data/`. Never edit them by hand; copy them again
  when the mock data changes.

## UI

- Interface text is Russian, in plain words a mechanic understands.
- Mobile-first: works from 320px wide without horizontal scrolling.
- Interactive targets are at least `--control-height` (44px) tall.
- Prefer native controls; every input has a visible label; errors and
  warnings use `Notice`.
- Visual language: service-bay diagnostics — graphite header, steel
  page background, amber signal accent from the logo, IBM Plex Sans /
  Mono via Google Fonts (fallbacks remain). Keep one soft elevation
  shadow; do not switch to purple themes, dark mode as default, glow
  stacks, or pill-heavy chrome. Prefer CSS-only visual polish over
  markup/logic changes.

## Workflow and Verification

- Requirements chain: customer/TZ docs in `docs/` →
  `docs/MVP-требования.md` → `docs/frontend-checkpoints.md`.
  Work follows the checkpoints file: one checkpoint at a time, in order;
  start the next one only on the user's command. If requirements change,
  update MVP first, then the checkpoints — never the other way around.
- Run `npm run typecheck` and `npm run build` in `frontend/`.
- Check behaviour in the browser at desktop width and at 320px.
- `npm run preview` serves the production build on
  `http://localhost:4173`. It is a different origin, so its ticket
  history is separate from the dev server's.
- Dev server: `http://localhost:5173`. `127.0.0.1:5173` does not work,
  because Vite binds to `localhost` only.
- For verification always use the local mock: ML on `127.0.0.1:8001`,
  TOIR on `127.0.0.1:8002`. Corporate stand URLs in the user's `.env`
  need the company VPN and are unavailable to the agent.
